<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- Bluetooth (OBD dongle) -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH" android:maxSdkVersion="30" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN"
        android:usesPermissionFlags="neverForLocation" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_ADMIN"
        android:maxSdkVersion="30" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />

    <!-- Background location (`.scratch/location-intelligence/map.md`, settled decision 11). Kept
         separate from the foreground pair above because Android enforces the same split at grant
         time: this can only be requested AFTER foreground is already granted, and from API 30 the
         system will not even offer it in a normal runtime dialog - the driver is sent to Settings'
         "Allow all the time" picker by hand. See `location/BackgroundLocationAccess.kt` for the
         three-state resolution this makes necessary and `ui/SettingsRows.kt`'s LocationAccessRow
         for how a refusal is worded rather than hidden.

         WHY IT'S NEEDED, NOT JUST WHY IT'S ANNOYING: geofences (place reminders, garage-on-approach)
         are registered with the OS and do not fire at all without this - `GeofencingClient`
         silently drops PendingIntents the app has no standing background permission to receive.
         Hazard checks (NWS/USGS/NIFC raises) are worse without it in a quieter way: they'd only run
         while LEGION is in the foreground, which is precisely useless for a category whose whole
         point is acting when Kevin is NOT looking at his phone - a tornado warning that only reaches
         him if he happens to have the app open is not a safety feature.

         Costs a real battery conversation (map decision 16 notes the Samsung sleeping-apps risk and
         accepts it knowingly) and a worse permission-dialog flow than any other permission in this
         file. **Sideloaded, never Play-listed** (same posture as READ_CALL_LOG above) - Play's
         background-location review policy, which would otherwise require a declared-use form and
         justification video, does not apply to an app that is never submitted. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <!-- The Quiet tile (2026-09-27): LEGION's own Do Not Disturb mode needs DND access, which the
         user grants on a system screen. Without it the tile says so and changes nothing. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NOTIFICATION_POLICY" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!-- Dumb mode's alarm-stream fallback (ADR 0062): a chosen person's message vibrates when the
         system will not let a notification channel break through Do Not Disturb. A normal permission. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_PHONE_STATE" />

    <!-- Caller ID and voice call control (2026-08-21, Kevin: "i wanna know whos calling ... can i
         also pick it up or decline via voice?"). All three are runtime-granted and asked for
         together on the Setup screen, because they are one feature to a human.

         READ_CALL_LOG is the ONLY thing that populates the caller number. Verified against AOSP
         rather than assumed: TelephonyRegistry computes
         `String phoneNumberOrEmpty = r.canReadCallLog() ? phoneNumber : ""` per callback delivery,
         so without it PhoneStateListener hands us an EMPTY STRING, not null - which is why the old
         announcement could only ever say "a call is ringing". READ_PHONE_NUMBERS does NOT help; it
         is the local SIM's own number, not the caller's.

         It is a HARD RESTRICTED permission (permissionFlags="hardRestricted" in the platform
         manifest), so the installer must allowlist it. `adb install` and Android Studio do by
         default (PackageManagerShellCommand passes INSTALL_ALL_WHITELIST_RESTRICTED_PERMISSIONS).
         **This is the one line here that would block a Play listing** - Play requires default-dialer
         status for call-log permissions - and LEGION is sideloaded and never listed, so it does not
         bite. If that ever changes, CallScreeningService is the documented alternative, at the cost
         of taking an exclusive system role off Samsung's Smart Call.

         DO NOT "modernise" TelephonyController to TelephonyCallback.CallStateListener. Its
         onCallStateChanged has no number parameter at all - AOSP's own dispatch comment says "The
         new callback does NOT provide the phone number" - so the migration would silently delete
         this feature. PhoneStateListener is deprecated and still works.

         READ_CONTACTS turns that number into a name via ContactsContract.PhoneLookup.
         ANSWER_PHONE_CALLS is an ordinary dangerous permission - no role, no default-dialer
         requirement - and is all TelecomManager.acceptRingingCall/endCall need. Both are deprecated
         since API 29 and both still work. Neither can touch a SELF-MANAGED call (WhatsApp, Signal,
         Teams): Telecom silently ignores those for a non-privileged caller, and the tool
         descriptions say so rather than failing mysteriously. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CALL_LOG" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CONTACTS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ANSWER_PHONE_CALLS" />
    <!-- place_call (ticket 26): CALL_PHONE is what lets ACTION_CALL dial directly instead of
         only opening the dialer for the user to press call themselves. Runtime-requested, same
         as the three above; PlaceCallAction treats it missing as its own honest failure. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CALL_PHONE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CONNECTED_DEVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PLAYBACK" />

    <!-- Android Auto probe harness (`.scratch/android-auto/map.md`, research 04). Required by BOTH
         microphone routing paths ticket 04 found (Telecom's MODE_IN_COMMUNICATION wrapper and the
         plain-foreground-service MODE_IN_COMMUNICATION + setCommunicationDevice route), so it is
         added now rather than per-wave even though wave 1 does not yet touch audio routing. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS" />

    <!-- Wave 2's self-managed call probe (ticket 14). Required by TelecomManager.registerPhoneAccount
         for a PhoneAccount with CAPABILITY_SELF_MANAGED - see CarCallProbe.registerAccount and
         .car.LegionConnectionService below. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_OWN_CALLS" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_WIFI_STATE" />

    <!-- Local alarms for the notes/lists/calendar domain
         (`.scratch/notes-lists-calendar/issues/03-android-alarm-mechanism.md`). SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM
         only (revocable, requested at runtime) - NOT USE_EXACT_ALARM, rejected on proportionality
         in that ticket's Answer. RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED re-arms reminders after a reboot, since
         AlarmManager does not persist alarms across one. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!-- Google Calendar permissions REMOVED - one-today ticket 01, "cut Google entirely"
         (2026-09-01, `.scratch/one-today/issues/01-one-agenda-source.md`). READ_CALENDAR/
         WRITE_CALENDAR (ticket 13 read, ticket 14 write) backed the retired
         `calendar/CalendarProvider.kt`'s live `CalendarContract` read/write - LEGION's calendar is
         its own local `events` table now, which needs no runtime permission at all. The 261 rows
         that arrived through the old import stay (their `googleEventId` column is provenance, not
         a live link); there is nothing left here for either permission to gate. -->

    <!-- Package visibility (API 30+). Without this the Spotify app is invisible to us:
         SpotifyController.isInstalled always reports false, and App Remote's own bind to
         com.spotify.music fails, regardless of what client ID is saved or authorized.
         The SDK requires it; this app had never declared it (fixed 2026-08-12 with the
         Setup screen). Browsers are queried for SpotifyWebApi.beginAuthorization's
         ACTION_VIEW hop to Spotify's approval page. -->
    <queries>
        <!-- The app drawer (ADR 0050) lists every launchable app. Without this, package
             visibility on API 30+ hides most of them. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent>
        <!-- Dumb mode (ADR 0062) finds the camera app by who answers this intent, never by a
             hard-coded package. The MAIN/LAUNCHER query above does not cover it. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.media.action.STILL_IMAGE_CAMERA" />
        </intent>
        <package android:name="com.google.android.apps.maps" />
        <package android:name="com.spotify.music" />
        <!-- Retro-games ticket 03: the three emulators LEGION hands a game to; without these the
             "is it installed" check reads every one as missing on API 30+. -->
        <package android:name="com.retroarch.aarch64" />
        <package android:name="me.magnum.melonds" />
        <package android:name="org.ppsspp.ppsspp" />
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="https" />
        </intent>
        <!-- Books read-aloud (ADR 0061): without this, API 30+ hides the installed text-to-speech
             engines and Listen would report "no engine" on a phone that has one. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.TTS_SERVICE" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:name=".MidnightApplication"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Legion">

        <!-- Android Auto's media-capability declaration (research 02 section 1a,
             `.scratch/android-auto/map.md`). Points at res/xml/automotive_app_desc.xml, whose
             single <uses name="media"/> line is what puts LEGION in Android Auto's media source
             list. Deliberately NOT com.android.automotive - that is the Android Automotive OS
             declaration and is out of scope (map's "Out of scope" section). -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.gms.car.application"
            android:resource="@xml/automotive_app_desc" />

        <!-- The phone's assistant (2026-09-27): holding the side key with LEGION as the digital
             assistant app lands here, NOT on MainActivity. It may show over the lock screen only
             to ask for the unlock prompt, and draws nothing, so no app data ever appears on a
             locked phone. See AssistActivity's doc for the parked-request bug this closes.
             VOICE_COMMAND is the older headset/button equivalent. NOT noHistory: the unlock
             prompt covers this activity, and noHistory discards it right then, so the "unlocked"
             callback never arrived and the conversation never started. Every path finish()es
             itself instead. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ui.AssistActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:showWhenLocked="true"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.ASSIST" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VOICE_COMMAND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!-- The app's entry point, and the phone's home app when Kevin picks it (ADR 0050,
             2026-09-27, reversing CLAUDE.md's "Not a launcher"). The CATEGORY_HOME filter below
             only makes it SELECTABLE in Settings > Default apps > Home app; nothing here forces it.
             singleTask matters twice: the Spotify OAuth redirect (below), and a Home press, which
             must reach the existing instance via onNewIntent rather than stack a new one.
             No adjustResize (web-assistant ticket 09 added it for a typed box on the bottom bar,
             2026-10-04; removed 2026-10-05 by the chat panel): the text field now lives in a
             bottom sheet, its own window, which handles the keyboard itself. Resizing THIS window
             as well would make the strip jump under the sheet. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ui.MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:screenOrientation="unspecified"
            android:resizeableActivity="true"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|navigation|uiMode"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Legion">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.HOME" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Spotify App Remote's app-fixed OAuth redirect (SpotifyController.REDIRECT_URI).
                 Must match this scheme+host verbatim; the driver enters the same URI as the
                 Redirect URI in their own Spotify dev app. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="com.kevin.legion" android:host="spotify-callback" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- "Hey Google, ask LEGION X" (research 02 section 1d,
                 developer.android.com/training/cars/media/voice-actions). Landing this on
                 MainActivity rather than a dedicated activity keeps wave 1 to scaffolding only -
                 what LEGION actually DOES with a play-from-search intent is later-wave/ticket 08
                 work; nothing here reads this intent's extras yet. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.media.action.MEDIA_PLAY_FROM_SEARCH" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!-- SavedPlacesActivity, LedgerImportActivity, PantryImportActivity all
             DELETED (ticket 07 resolution §5). Their content is absorbed into
             MainActivity's single NavHost as fleet/places, ledger/import, and
             pantry/import - only the hosting changed, so there is no longer a
             second <activity> for any of them. -->

        <!-- WidgetPagerActivity DELETED (cutover 5, docs/architecture/cutover5-2026-08-24.md).
             The widget pager it hosted (WidgetPagerRoot) is now MainActivity's own start
             destination (composable(LegionRoute.DASHBOARD)), the app's home - same absorption
             SavedPlacesActivity/LedgerImportActivity/PantryImportActivity got at ticket 07, see
             that comment above: only the hosting changed, the composable is unchanged. -->

        <!-- The mirror/sync settings stub (aspect-engine ticket 20 item 5) - same debug-entry
             posture as WidgetPagerActivity above: exported=false, no launcher intent-filter,
             reached only via
             `adb shell am start -n com.kevin.legion/.ui.mirror.MirrorSyncActivity`
             until a later ticket folds it into MainActivity's own NavHost. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ui.mirror.MirrorSyncActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="Mirror Sync"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Legion" />

        <!-- Books reader (ADR 0061). A FragmentActivity because Readium's EPUB navigator is a
             Fragment; configChanges so a rotation never destroys the page being read. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".books.ReaderActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="Reader"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|navigation|uiMode"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Legion" />

        <!-- Read-aloud keeps going with the screen off as a media session, so the lock screen has
             play and pause (Books, ADR 0061). Not exported: only this app's notification controller
             binds it. The media3 action is how SessionToken recognises the service type. -->
        <service
            android:name=".books.BookReadAloudService"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="mediaPlayback">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="androidx.media3.session.MediaSessionService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <service
            android:name=".service.AriaForegroundService"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="connectedDevice|dataSync|microphone" />

        <!-- Android Auto probe harness wave 1 (`.scratch/android-auto/map.md`, research 02
             section 1c) - a STUB media3 MediaLibraryService, VISIBILITY PROBE ONLY, see
             LegionMediaLibraryService's own doc comment for exactly what it does and does not do.
             exported=true so an external client (Android Auto) can bind. Both actions declared so
             legacy MediaBrowserCompat clients (Android Auto is one) can bind alongside media3
             clients - see research 02's own citation for why both are needed. -->
        <service
            android:name=".car.LegionMediaLibraryService"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true"
            android:foregroundServiceType="mediaPlayback">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="androidx.media3.session.MediaSessionService" />
                <action android:name="android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- Wave 2's self-managed call probe (ticket 14). BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE is what
             stops any app other than the system Telecom process from binding this - required by
             the platform for a ConnectionService, per the same shape as any other bound-service
             permission gate in this file. exported implied true by the intent-filter + permission
             pair (Telecom binds cross-process), same reasoning as MediaNotificationListener below. -->
        <service
            android:name=".car.LegionConnectionService"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.telecom.ConnectionService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- Grants MediaSessionManager access to active media sessions (now-playing
             state, e.g. Spotify) once the driver enables notification access in
             system settings. This comment used to end "No notifications are parsed."; since dumb
             mode (ADR 0062, 2026-10-22) the listener also reads WHO a notification is from while
             that mode is on, to hold all but a chosen person's. It stores no notification content. -->
        <service
            android:name=".service.MediaNotificationListener"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- BootReceiver came BACK for phase 2a (ticket 03), but deliberately without the old
             startActivity(MainActivity) behaviour the ticket-07-deleted predecessor had - see
             BootReceiver's own doc comment. Its only job is re-arming scheduled reminders, since
             AlarmManager forgets everything across a reboot. exported=true: BOOT_COMPLETED is sent
             by the system, not by this app. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".service.BootReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Fires on either direction of SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM's grant state changing - ticket 03's
             finding that revoking it kills the process and cancels every pending exact alarm, and
             this broadcast is the only recovery path. exported=true: sent by the system. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".service.ExactAlarmPermissionReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.app.action.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM_PERMISSION_STATE_CHANGED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Fired only by this app's own PendingIntents (AlarmScheduler's alarm, and the
             notification's SNOOZE/DONE actions) - never by the system, so exported=false. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".service.ReminderAlarmReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />
        <receiver
            android:name=".service.ReminderActionReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!-- Fired only by DatesAlarmScheduler's own single PendingIntent, and the reminder
             notification's SILENCE action - never by the system, so exported=false, same
             reasoning as ReminderAlarmReceiver/ReminderActionReceiver above (aspect-engine
             ticket 19, the Dates aspect build). -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".service.DatesReminderAlarmReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />
        <receiver
            android:name=".service.DatesReminderActionReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!-- Fired only by WellbeingDigestScheduler's own PendingIntent - never by the system, so
             exported=false. goal-plans ticket 05's one alarm for the scheduled wellbeing digest. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".wellbeing.WellbeingDigestAlarmReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!-- Login-expiry notices: the alarm is this app's own PendingIntent and the Stop action is
             this app's own notification button - never the system, so exported=false. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".freshness.LoginExpiryAlarmReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />
        <receiver
            android:name=".freshness.LoginExpiryStopReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!-- Fired only by GeofencingClient delivering to GeofenceManager's own PendingIntent
             (location-intelligence ticket 05) - never by the system directly, so exported=false,
             same reasoning as ReminderAlarmReceiver above. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".location.GeofenceBroadcastReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!-- Fired only by the ring-time notification's own ANSWER/DECLINE PendingIntents
             (command-center ticket 05, ADR 0035's founding case) - never by the system, so
             exported=false, same reasoning as ReminderActionReceiver above. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".service.CallNotificationReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!-- FileProvider for CameraCapture's camera-app handoff URI (pantry
             receipt photos). Authority = packageName + ".provider". Not
             exported; the OS broker grants read-URI permission to the
             receiving camera app for the duration of the capture only. -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.provider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/provider_paths" />
        </provider>

        <!-- The Quiet tile: vibrate, calls and alarms only, every other notification silent.
             See quiet/QuietMode.kt. -->
        <!-- Dumb mode's call screener (ADR 0062). Android binds it only after the user grants LEGION the
             call screening role on the setup screen; that role displaces Samsung Smart Call. It only
             ever silences a ring, never rejects or disconnects a call. -->
        <service
            android:name=".dumb.DumbCallScreeningService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_SCREENING_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.telecom.CallScreeningService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- Dumb mode's bounce (ADR 0062): window-state changes only, no window content (see
             res/xml/dumb_bounce_service.xml). Android runs it only after the user turns it on in
             Accessibility settings; a sideloaded build first needs "Allow restricted settings" under
             App info. It sends a hidden app home and shows DumbBounceActivity. -->
        <service
            android:name=".dumb.DumbBounceService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="Dumb mode"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_ACCESSIBILITY_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.accessibilityservice.AccessibilityService" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.accessibilityservice"
                android:resource="@xml/dumb_bounce_service" />
        </service>

        <!-- The "Dumb mode is on" screen a bounced app leaves behind. Not exported: only the bounce
             service starts it. Out of Recents so it does not linger as a way back to anything. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ui.dumb.DumbBounceActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:label="Dumb mode"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Legion" />

        <!-- The dumb phone mode tile (ADR 0062): off to on in one tap; leaving is never a tile tap. -->
        <service
            android:name=".dumb.DumbModeTileService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:icon="@drawable/ic_dumb_tile"
            android:label="Dumb mode"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.quicksettings.action.QS_TILE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <service
            android:name=".quiet.QuietTileService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:icon="@drawable/ic_quiet_tile"
            android:label="Quiet"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.quicksettings.action.QS_TILE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.service.quicksettings.TOGGLEABLE_TILE"
                android:value="true" />
        </service>
    </application>

</manifest>
